      "dom_id": "question-160571",
      "problem": "What is the distance between the points $(-3,-2)$  and $(-3,-7)?$",
      "worked_solution": "**Method 1** \n\nWe plot the given points in the coordinate plane.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14220930/assets/fa516432771fd242ed51ba8548dd04ca88991fc14992be811a53f8e124b9ff7d.png)\n\nThe distance between two points is the length of the line segment that connects them.\n\nFrom the diagram, we see that the length of the segment is $5$ units, so the distance equals $5.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14220930/assets/999cdc70474e5b928b28d611d4c66fb3eeea5716f516af41574712337cb6f9e9.png)\n\n**Method 2**\n\nWe can find the distance between the two points by finding the distance of each point from the $x$-axis and then subtracting.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14220930/assets/91bd9f4cd88e966b796f97184eee8aa791e2f20301ff700906ded4d4731eaaf7.png)\n\nThe point $(-3,-7)$ is at a distance of $7$ from the $x$-axis, and the point $(-3,-2)$ is at a distance of $2$ from the $x$-axis. Therefore, the distance between the two points is $7-2=5.$",
      "result": "Incorrect",
